   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, in relation to instituting additional ballot\naccountability practices",
    "basePrintNo": "S5129",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5129-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §7-105, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5129\n\nSPONSOR: MYRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the election law, in relation to\ninstituting additional ballot accountability practices\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to enhance ballot-design and usability.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill adds a new § 7-105 to the election law to provide\nthat numbered and perforated ballot stubs shall not be required if the\nState Board of Elections implements a ballot accountability process,\nwhich: 1.) accounts for the number of each ballot style received by an\nelection inspector, 2.) retains a running count of ballots distributed\nand spoiled, and 3.) verifies at the close of polls that the number of\nballots distributed to voters, including any spoiled ballots, when added\nto the unvoted ballots, equals the total number of ballots received by\nan inspector for the election.\n\nThis section further provides that a ballot consisting of two or more\nsheets does not need to be divided by a line of perforation if:  the\nballots clearly inform the voter of the number of sheets the voter is\nentitled to, and each sheet is prominently numbered.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York law, uniquely, requires ballot stubs (a numbered stub that is\nseparated from the ballot at the time of delivery to the voter) and\nperforated ballots such that when the ballot requires two sheets the\nvoter is given one long ballot divided by a perforation which the voter\nmust tear to produce two sheets that can be separately placed through\nthe ballot scanner and into the ballot box. These provisions are\ndesigned to ensure ballot accountability as to the number of ballots\nused and to ensure that a voter receives a complete set of ballots.\n\nBallot on demand systems are often incompatible with ballot stubs and\nthe requirement that multi-sheet ballots be produced on a single piece\nof perforated paper because few device printers can print on such large\nsheets. Moreover, the experience of New York City in 2018 with perfo-\nrated ballots strongly suggests this ballot configuration causes more\nscanner jams imperiling the orderly conduct of an election.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
